{"name":"Soapstone","hex":"#ece5da","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soapstone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft rock, rich in talc, also containing serpentine and either magnetite, dolomite or calcite.","Synonym of saponite."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To scrub with soapstone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soapstone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soapstone","blocks":[{"text":"Soapstone is a talc-schist, which is a type of metamorphic rock. It is composed largely of the magnesium-rich mineral talc. It is produced by dynamothermal metamorphism and metasomatism, which occur in subduction zones, changing rocks by heat and pressure, with influx of fluids but without melting. It has been a carving medium for thousands of years."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Soapstone_%28Speckstein%29_-_several_colored_samples.jpg/330px-Soapstone_%28Speckstein%29_-_several_colored_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soapstone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Talc_block.jpg/330px-Talc_block.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Talc_block.jpg/330px-Talc_block.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A block of talc","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATalc_block.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Mythological_figures_by_Kayasark%2C_Inuit%2C_Pelly_Bay%2C_Canada%2C_steatite%2C_HAA.JPG/500px-Mythological_figures_by_Kayasark%2C_Inuit%2C_Pelly_Bay%2C_Canada%2C_steatite%2C_HAA.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Mythological_figures_by_Kayasark%2C_Inuit%2C_Pelly_Bay%2C_Canada%2C_st